README-BUSERR -- programs that fault, and how to run them first===============================================================A program that stops with    Exit code: E_PRCABT(228) Process Aborted    Exception: pid=2 vector=$02 err=#000:102has taken a bus error: vector $02 on a 68000, a read from an address themachine would not give it.  The process is killed, so nothing is printed.Before believing it, run the program from a login session.  SYS/loginsets TERM and TERMCAP, and a program that reads TERMCAP and finds nonemay follow the missing pointer and fault.  Run as os9exec's firstprocess, with no environment:  aterm, snake        take bus errors  cribbage            "Environment variable TERM not defined", aborts  crib                "Unknown terminal type ''", abortsAfter `bash /dd/SYS/login' all four work.  Under os9exec a hostvariable crosses into the OS-9 environment only when its name startswith `@': `@TERM=vt100' and `@TERMCAP=...' on the os9exec command lineare enough for aterm to print its banner.  A plain TERM does nothing.A fault on the vector a program asked to handle means the handler didnot go where it asked.  `config' installs its own line-F handler (vector$0B) to do double-precision arithmetic in software, and needs nocoprocessor.  os9exec builds before 2026-09-12 placed that handler 64Koff and `config' died where `double' began; on a current os9exec itruns to its last line.The SNOBOL4 games-----------------  poker  blackjak  rpoem  rstory  stoneThese read their syntax files from /dd/GAMES/SNOBOL (PHRASE.SYN,RPOEM.SYN, RSTORY.SYN, RSTORY.ACT), so they run from any directory.Without those files they fault with `Illegal instruction: 4afc'.DOC/snobol has the originals.aterm-----`aterm' is a terminal program in 68000 assembly.  Its configuration isin SYS/ATERM:    ATerm.Ctl     control file, ANSI/VT-100 sequences, driver `term'    ATerm.Phone   dialler list, empty    ATerm.Print   printer scriptDOC/aterm/ATerm.Doc is the manual and SRC/aterm/ATerm.a the source.  Itneeds a serial port: `aterm /t1'.